<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tokugawa Shogunate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tokugawa Bakufu, Tokugawa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tokugawa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">Shogunate</a>, or Tokugawa Bakufu, was the military government that ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> from 1603 to 1868 under shoguns of the Tokugawa family, with its seat at Edo. It imposed a long peace, regulated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>, restricted foreign contact and presided over economic and urban growth and a flourishing popular culture. Its fall in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a> led to the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Shogunate" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maproom · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Japan_in_Provinces_in_time_of_Iyeyasu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Meiji Restoration of January 1868 overthrew the <b>Tokugawa shogunate</b> after some 265 years and restored Emperor Meiji to nominal supreme power, while the Charter Oath of April 1868 committed Japan to seeking knowledge throughout the world and abandoning the customs of the feudal past.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Japan Confucian ideas arrived with Chinese writing and learning by the 6th century; under the <b>Tokugawa shogunate</b> the Neo-Confucianism of Zhu Xi became the official teaching of the shogunate’s academy and was taught in schools for the samurai.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Tokugawa shogunate</b>, Confucian scholars and the National Learning movement of Motoori Norinaga and Hirata Atsutane exalted the kami and the emperor as the essence of Japan.</blockquote><a href="/religion/shinto">Shinto · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>From the south came the Matsumae clan, to whom the <b>Tokugawa shogunate</b> gave exclusive rights to trade with the Ainu.</blockquote><a href="/culture/ainu">Ainu · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Historian Marius B. Jansen calls it a system of “feudal autonomy”, in which the <b>Tokugawa shogunate</b> left the daimyō to run their own domains.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>East of the Elbe lords tied peasants more tightly to the land in a second serfdom from the 16th century, and in Japan the <b>Tokugawa shogunate</b> ruled from 1603 to 1868 through some 260 domain lords, whose domains were abolished in 1871.</blockquote><a href="/government/feudalism">Feudalism · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
